                            <a name="comment2444"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Good Writer
</h4>

<p>
I don't think being a good person can make one a good writer -- or even a better writer. But I see the point -- that the good writer is true to reality. And if it is assumed that the good person is also thus, it follows that the good person would be a good writer. But beyond intentions, writing is about two things: Reporting and craft. I'd guess there are lots of truly nice people who can't write a lick. I know a few of them. <br />
<br />
But becoming a more complete person? That makes you a better writer because it makes you a better reporter. Six years ago, I got married. Almost four years ago, my baby girl was born. Two years ago, the business my parents spent their adult lives building failed miserably. I have no doubt that these things make me a better writer because they make me/allow me to see things in a way I was not able to before. I know what it's like to argue with my wife and then go sleep in the same bed with her. I know what it's like to watch a fully formed human being that looks just like me enter the world. I know what it's like to listen to my mom cry and know there's nothing I can do to make it all better. All of these things -- and lots of other things --help me empathize. Empathy, not character, is the key to good writing. <br />
<br />
As far as the improving the craft goes, none of this helps. It seems only reading and lots of writing can do that.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


