                            <a name="comment2445"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Good Writer
</h4>

<p>
Much as I respect John Gardner -- and agree that "The Art of Fiction" ought to be on every writer's bookshelf -- I have to conclude reluctantly that moral character has very little to do with the quality of what a writer produces. <br />
Plagiarism aside, of course, there is hardly any crime up to, and including, murder that some great writer hasn't committed at one time or another. Marlowe and Jean Genet spring to mind, but there are dozens of others. In fact, one could argue that a touch or two of immorality gives a writer's work a certain resonance and depth. This is not a recommendation, simply an observation. <br />
It would be nice if writing talent were distributed according to merit, but it's not, any more than the talent to paint or perform music. We in then press often promote this childish belief when we mourn the death of some great talented scoundrel as though it were the passing of a saint.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


