                            <a name="comment2448"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The Good Writer
</h4>

<p>
When I was a graduate student, I studied medieval literature and bumped into the work of John Gardner, who was a scholar on the way to becoming a novelist.  I remember leading a discussion of his early novel "Grendel," Beowulf from the monster's point of view.  Cool book. But my teachers who knew Gardner as a scholar thought he was a bit of a scoundrel for reasons I now forget.<br />
<br />
I share the opinion that being a good person has little to do with being a good writer; but I also think there is a way in which being a good writer can make you a better person under certain conditions.<br />
<br />
Just as artists or engineers or doctors see the world through the lens of their profession, so the writer comes to see the world through the lens of craft -- and story.  This leads inevitably toward character, toward empathy, toward a nuanced view of the world.  Then again, maybe not. 
</p>
